\h Matthew
\toc1 The Good News According to Matthew
\toc2 Matthew
\toc3 Mat
\mt1 Matthew
\c 17
\s1 The temple tax
\p
\v 24 \w When|strong="G1161"\w* \w they|strong="G1161"\w* had come to Capernaum, those who collected the didrachma coins\f + \fr 17:24 \ft A didrachma is a Greek silver coin worth 2 drachmas.\f* came to Peter.
\v 25 He said, “Yes.” When he came into the house, Jesus anticipated him.
\v 26 Peter said to him, “From strangers.” Jesus said to him, “Therefore the children are exempt.
\v 27 But, lest we cause them to stumble, go to the sea, cast a hook, and take up the first fish.”
\c 18
\p
\v 1 In that hour the disciples came to Jesus, saying, “Who then is greatest in the Kingdom of Heaven?”
\v 2 Jesus called a little child to himself, and set him in the middle of them
\v 3 and said, \wj “Most certainly I tell you, unless you turn and become as little children, you will in no way enter into the Kingdom of Heaven.\wj*
\v 4 \wj Whoever therefore humbles himself as this little child is the greatest in the Kingdom of Heaven.\wj*
\v 5 \wj Whoever receives one such little child in my name receives me.\wj*
\s1 Causes of stumbling
\p
\v 6-7 \wj Whoever causes one of these little ones who believe in me to stumble, it would be better for him if a huge millstone were hung around his neck. Woe to the world because of occasions of stumbling!\wj*\x + \xo 18:6 \xt Mark 9:42\x*
\v 8 \wj If your hand or your foot causes you to stumble, cut it off and cast it from you.\wj*
\v 9 \wj If your eye causes you to stumble, pluck it out and cast it from you.\wj*
\p
\v 10 \wj See that you don’t despise one of these little ones.\wj*
\v 11 \wj For the Son of Man came to save that which was lost.\wj*
\s1 The parable of the lost sheep
\p
\v 12 \wj What do you think? If a man has one hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray, doesn’t he leave the ninety-nine?\wj*
\v 13 \wj If he finds it, most certainly he rejoices over it more than over the ninety-nine.\wj*
\v 14 \wj Even so it is not the will of your Father who is in heaven that one of these little ones should perish.\wj*
\s1 The parable of the unforgiving servant
\p
\v 21 Then Peter came and said to him, “Lord, how often shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? Until seven times?”
\p
\v 22 Jesus said to him, \wj “I don’t tell you until seven times, but, until seventy times seven.\wj*
\v 23 \wj Therefore the Kingdom of Heaven is like a certain king, who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.\wj*
\v 24 \wj When he had begun to settle, one was brought to him who owed him ten thousand talents.\wj*\f + \fr 18:24 \ft Ten thousand talents represents an extremely large sum of money.\f*
\v 25 \wj But because he couldn’t pay, his lord commanded him to be sold.\wj*
\v 26 \wj The servant therefore fell down and knelt before him, saying, ‘Lord, have patience with me, and I will repay you all!’\wj*
\v 27 \wj The lord of that servant, being moved with compassion, released him and forgave him the debt.\wj*
\p
\v 28 \wj “But that servant went out and found one of his fellow servants who owed him one hundred denarii.\wj*
\v 29 \wj “So his fellow servant fell down at his feet and begged him, saying, ‘Have patience with me, and I will repay you!’\wj*
\v 30 \wj He would not, but went and cast him into prison until he should pay back that which was due.\wj*
\v 31 \wj So when his fellow servants saw what was done, they were exceedingly sorry.\wj*
\v 32 \wj Then his lord called him in and said to him, ‘You wicked servant! I forgave you all that debt.\wj*
\v 33 \wj Shouldn’t you also have had mercy on your fellow servant, even as I had mercy on you?’\wj*
\v 34 \wj His lord was angry, and delivered him to the tormentors.\wj*
\v 35 \wj So my heavenly Father will also do to you, if you don’t each forgive your brother from your hearts for his misdeeds.”\wj*
\c 19
\s1 Teaching about marriage
\p
\v 1 When Jesus had finished these words, he departed from Galilee.
\v 2 Great multitudes followed him, and he healed them there.
\s1 Questions on the road
\p
\v 3 Pharisees came to him, testing him.
\v 4 He answered, \wj “Haven’t you read that he who made them from the beginning made them male and female?\wj*
\v 5 \wj and said, ‘For this cause a man shall leave his father and mother, and shall be joined to his wife.’\wj*
\v 6 \wj So that they are no more two, but one flesh.\wj*
\v 7 They asked him, “Why then did Moses command us to give her a certificate of divorce?”
\v 8 He said to them, \wj “Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because of the hardness of your hearts.\wj*
\v 9 \wj I tell you that whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, commits adultery.\wj*
\p
\v 10 His disciples said to him, “If this is the case of the man with his wife, it is not expedient to marry.”
\v 11 But he said to them, \wj “Not all men can receive this saying.\wj*
\v 12 \wj For there are eunuchs who were born that way.\wj*
\p
\v 13 Then little children were brought to him that he should lay his hands on them.
\v 14 But Jesus said, \wj “Allow the little children, and don’t forbid them to come to me.\wj*
\v 15 He laid his hands on them, and departed from there.
\p
\v 16 Behold, one came to him and said, “Good teacher, what good thing shall I do, that I may have eternal life?”
\v 17 He said to him, \wj “Why do you call me good? No one is good but one, that is, God.\wj*
\v 18 He said to him, “Which ones?” Jesus said, \wj “‘You shall not murder.’\wj*
\v 19 \wj ‘Honor your father and your mother.’\wj*
\v 20 The young man said to him, “All these things I have observed from my youth. What do I still lack?”
